<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sada Kaur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1762–1832</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sada Kaur was the leader of the Kanhaiya <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/misl">Misl</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-confederacy">Sikh confederacies</a>, after the death of her husband Gurbakhsh Singh in 1785. She married her daughter to the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> of the Sukerchakia Misl and supported him with troops, notably in his capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a> in 1799. Ranjit Singh later took over her lands, and she died in confinement in 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sada-kaur.jpg" alt="Sada Kaur" width="320" height="406" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kehar Singh, court artist of Maharaja Ranjit Singh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_Sketch_of_Sardarni_Sada_Kaur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
